      "worked": "A rhombus's diagonals bisect each other at right angles. For $d_1,d_2>0$, each side joins the ends of two perpendicular half-diagonals. The distance formula gives the side length, and the four right triangles give the area:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2&=\\left(\\frac{d_1}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{d_2}{2}\\right)^2\\\\P&=4x\\\\P&=2\\sqrt{d_1^2+d_2^2}\\\\A&=4\\times\\frac12\\times\\frac{d_1}{2}\\times\\frac{d_2}{2}\\\\A&=\\frac{d_1d_2}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nCompare two rhombi using the same length units.\n\nFor $d_1=6$ and $d_2=8$, $x=5$, $P=20$ and $A=24$.\n\nFor $d_1=d_2=5\\sqrt2$, $x=5$, $P=20$ and $A=25$.\n\nThe ratios $\\frac{P}{A}$ are $\\frac{20}{24}=\\frac{5}{6}$ and $\\frac{20}{25}=\\frac{4}{5}$, which differ. Thus there is no fixed constant $k$ with $P=kA$, so $P$ does not vary directly with $A$ for unrestricted rhombi.",
